        "id": "p1",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "In ancient China, when autumn came, people didn’t merely enjoy the cool breeze and falling leaves, but also got especially excited about one thing: eating crabs! For many ancient Chinese foodies, the perfect way to celebrate fall was with a plate of fresh crab and a cup of warm wine."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p2",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "According to traditional Chinese medicine, crab is a “cold” food that can "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "upset",
            "ref": "v-upset"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " the stomach when eaten alone. That’s why people paired it with warm wine. Together, they made a tasty and balanced combination. The feeling was so good that one poet wrote, “There’s no better joy in this world” than enjoying crabs and wine on a mountain top."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p3",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Many poets and scholars were huge crab lovers. The famous poet Su Dongpo once traded a poem to a friend in exchange for two crabs. Ouyang Xiu chose to retire near a lake simply because the crabs there were cheaper. The writer Li Yu was nicknamed the “Crab Fairy” because he could eat up to 20 crabs a day and even had a servant dedicated to cooking crabs for him! But the funniest story is about Qian Kun, a government official. When the emperor asked where he wanted to work, he replied, “Anywhere with crabs.”"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p4",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "In autumn, crab-themed parties were also popular among the upper class. In the classic novel "
          },
          {
            "t": "em",
            "v": "Dream of the Red Chamber"
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "",
            "zh": "《红楼梦》"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ", people hosted crab feasts in their gardens. They ate crabs with ginger and vinegar, drank hot wine, and wrote poems."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p5",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "During the Song dynasty, chefs invented new ways to prepare crabs. They made crab wontons, "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "crab roe soup buns",
            "zh": "蟹粉小笼包"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ", and even a creative dish called “crab-stuffed orange”. Later, in the Ming dynasty, people designed a special tool set called the “Eight Crab Tools”. It included scissors, "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "hooks",
            "ref": "v-hook"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ", and even a small hammer, allowing diners to open crab shells neatly and easily."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p6",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "To the ancient Chinese, crabs weren’t just a tasty seasonal delicacy. They were a symbol of autumn joy. Even today, crabs still hold a special place on Chinese dinner tables, continuing to bring happiness to food lovers."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
